The project was developed using multiple IoT platforms and cloud technologies to simulate a real-time server room monitoring system. Initially, the hardware setup was created in Wokwi, an online electronics simulation platform, where an ESP32 microcontroller was connected with various sensors such as the DHT22 temperature and humidity sensor, MQ2 gas sensor, PIR motion sensor, LDR light sensor, and an OLED display. The ESP32 continuously reads environmental data from these sensors and processes the values to determine the condition of the server room. During the first stage of development, the ESP32 transmitted sensor data using the MQTT communication protocol to the HiveMQ public broker. HiveMQ acted as a cloud-based MQTT message broker that received and distributed published sensor data to subscribed dashboards and monitoring tools. This architecture demonstrated real-time publish-subscribe communication commonly used in industrial IoT systems. Later, the system was upgraded by integrating the Blynk IoT platform to create a more interactive and professional cloud dashboard. The ESP32 connected to the Blynk cloud server through WiFi and continuously transmitted sensor data using virtual pins. The Blynk platform then displayed the received data on a real-time dashboard using gauges, labels, and indicators for temperature, humidity, gas levels, motion detection, and system status. Whenever abnormal conditions such as high temperature or dangerous gas levels were detected, the system automatically entered ALERT mode and updated both the local OLED display and the cloud dashboard. This complete workflow demonstrated real-time environmental monitoring, cloud communication, remote telemetry, and IoT dashboard integration for server room safety and infrastructure monitoring applications.
